1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It rises boldly over the Mississippi River.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
40,000 tons of steel and concrete.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
The biggest man-made arch in the world.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Nothing like this had ever been built before or attempted since.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Its simple and elegant form a testament to remarkable construction and engineering milestones.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, the St. Louis Arch on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:49,126 --> 00:00:50,600
The St. Louis Arch.

8
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:54,640
This spectacular structure overlooking the famed Mississippi River

9
00:00:54,641 --> 00:00:56,280
in the city of St. Louis, Missouri,

10
00:00:56,281 --> 00:01:00,620
is one of the most innovative arches and recognizable icons in the world.

11
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:05,600
Since 1967, when the arch was first built,

12
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:09,900
more than 65 million people from around the world have come to visit.

13
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:13,620
And for those who have seen it, the impression is lasting.

14
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:17,780
The St. Louis Arch, known officially as the Gateway Arch,

15
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:20,360
is America's tallest national monument

16
00:01:20,361 --> 00:01:23,680
and the only one commemorating America's westward expansion.

17
00:01:23,681 --> 00:01:27,900
And the arch itself is like a piece of outdoor sculpture, if you will.

18
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:35,020
It's a gigantic and very much a symbolic memorial to westward expansion

19
00:01:35,021 --> 00:01:38,320
because it takes the form of a literal gateway.

20
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:43,920
It's a portal through which people could literally pass if they wish.

21
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:51,800
It stands 630 feet tall, 25 feet taller than the Washington Monument,

22
00:01:51,801 --> 00:01:54,740
and more than twice as high as the Statue of Liberty.

23
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:59,820
One side of the arch faces due east, the other west,

24
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,860
so that its polished stainless steel exterior

25
00:02:02,861 --> 00:02:06,120
reflects both the rising and setting sun.

26
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:10,000
I don't think there's been any two days

27
00:02:10,001 --> 00:02:14,020
that I've gone down there that it's looked exactly the same.

28
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:17,420
The morning and evening light when it's pink

29
00:02:17,421 --> 00:02:19,740
and the way it shines off of it

30
00:02:19,741 --> 00:02:24,240
just gives these incredible colors that it reflects back.

31
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:26,100
It's a remarkable thing, I think.

32
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:27,680
It looks different from every angle.

33
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:29,880
When you're up close to it,

34
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:32,440
you can appreciate the incredible precision of the surfaces.

35
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:36,560
It's really something to just stand and look at it for long periods of time.

36
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:39,880
Deceptively simple in appearance,

37
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:43,200
the Gateway Arch is the result of exceptional design.

38
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:46,140
It is not a traditional Roman arch,

39
00:02:46,141 --> 00:02:47,880
but something entirely different,

40
00:02:47,881 --> 00:02:50,440
an inverted catenary curve.

41
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:55,500
An inverted catenary curve is the form that's made by a chain

42
00:02:55,501 --> 00:02:57,900
that's hung from two points and then let to drop.

43
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:00,140
So the form of the arch just reverses that.

44
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:02,220
It's kind of like a mirror image of that form.

45
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:07,980
The arch's 630-foot height is identically matched by its width.

46
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:12,900
It is also hollow, ingeniously supported by its outer core,

47
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:14,520
like a stalk of bamboo.

48
00:03:14,521 --> 00:03:18,520
The arch is composed really of two skins.

49
00:03:18,521 --> 00:03:25,520
The outer skin was made of the stainless steel on a steel plate,

50
00:03:25,521 --> 00:03:29,520
and the inner skin was made with carbon steel.

51
00:03:29,521 --> 00:03:34,520
And then the lower third of the arch is filled with concrete,

52
00:03:34,521 --> 00:03:39,520
and the upper two-thirds of the arch remain unfilled as a double skin.

53
00:03:39,521 --> 00:03:44,440
At the base of the arch, the walls are three feet thick.

54
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:47,500
They gradually taper to a mere seven inches.

55
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:59,460
Most people just kind of look at the outside and don't think of the fact that there's a unique stair system that's on the inside that goes all the way to the top on

56
00:03:59,461 --> 00:04:00,161
both sides,

57
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:07,000
that there's the whole tram system that's built in there with tracks and everything else that had to go in.

58
00:04:07,001 --> 00:04:17,300
Each year, nearly a million visitors take the trip inside to the top of the arch in a unique 80-passenger tram that is part elevator and part Ferris wheel.

59
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:22,760
There are two trams, one in each leg, operating independently.

60
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:29,660
For emergencies, there are also stairs, a flight of 1,076 steps.

61
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:36,540
The observation room at the top can comfortably hold about 100 people.

62
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,960
It measures 6 feet 9 inches high and 65 feet wide.

63
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:49,220
16 small windows line the east and west walls to provide visitors with expansive views.

64
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:53,620
To the west, the metropolis of St. Louis.

65
00:04:56,580 --> 00:05:00,060
To the east, the Mississippi River and the state of Illinois.

66
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:04,700
In good weather, visibility is 30 miles.

67
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,000
The arch was built to withstand winds of 150 miles per hour.

68
00:05:12,001 --> 00:05:20,000
It is also impervious to lightning strikes due to seven lightning rods, each a foot high affixed to its top.

69
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:29,000
What lies beneath the arch is a vast subterranean visitors complex with two large movie theaters

70
00:05:29,001 --> 00:05:34,000
and what's billed as the first underground museum in the United States.

71
00:05:34,001 --> 00:05:40,240
The arch itself is the centerpiece of the National Park Service's 100-acre Jefferson

72
00:05:40,241 --> 00:05:41,887
National Expansion Memorial.

73
00:05:41,888 --> 00:05:45,240
It is here on these very grounds where the village of St.

74
00:05:45,241 --> 00:05:51,620
Louis was founded in 1764 as a fur trading post, and where pivotal moments in America's

75
00:05:51,621 --> 00:05:57,520
history occurred.

76
00:05:57,521 --> 00:06:03,280
Two major rivers, the Mississippi River and the nearby Missouri River several miles west,

77
00:06:03,281 --> 00:06:07,440
made St. Louis a perfect weigh station for hunters and traders.

78
00:06:07,441 --> 00:06:09,480
Well, St. Louis, of course, has always been,

79
00:06:09,481 --> 00:06:11,560
probably since Native American times,

80
00:06:11,561 --> 00:06:14,160
a place of important juncture of rivers.

81
00:06:14,161 --> 00:06:15,600
And before there were railroads,

82
00:06:15,601 --> 00:06:18,120
that was the only way to travel long distances.

83
00:06:18,121 --> 00:06:19,960
So it was selected as the starting point

84
00:06:19,961 --> 00:06:22,840
for the Lewis and Clark expedition in 1804.

85
00:06:22,841 --> 00:06:25,440
This was just after Jefferson had negotiated

86
00:06:25,441 --> 00:06:28,560
the Louisiana Purchase from France,

87
00:06:28,561 --> 00:06:32,400
large territory which extended all the way up to Montana.

88
00:06:32,401 --> 00:06:40,480
Nothing was known about it, and so it was necessary to have an expedition that would go along the rivers and eventually end up in the Pacific Northwest.

89
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:44,863
There were less than a thousand people living in St.

90
00:06:44,864 --> 00:06:48,600
Louis when Meriwether Lewis and William Clark started their westward journey.

91
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:53,055
Yet only a few years after their return, St.

92
00:06:53,056 --> 00:06:56,240
Louis changed from Frontier Post to Boomtown.

93
00:06:56,241 --> 00:06:58,550
Soon you had entrepreneurs in St.

94
00:06:58,551 --> 00:07:03,660
Louis that became involved in all the aspects of the opening of the West,

95
00:07:03,661 --> 00:07:08,860
whether it be exploration or the various trade routes that existed,

96
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:13,620
particularly as soon as the Santa Fe Trail was opened in 1821.

97
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:20,623
A lot of the material goods and the rewards from those goods made their way to St.

98
00:07:20,624 --> 00:07:21,324
Louis.

99
00:07:21,325 --> 00:07:25,440
and most people who went on the overland route

100
00:07:25,441 --> 00:07:27,280
outfitted in St. Louis.

101
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:30,740
After the Civil War,

102
00:07:31,220 --> 00:07:34,040
St. Louis would continue to build its fortune on trade

103
00:07:34,041 --> 00:07:35,400
and the Mississippi River.

104
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:39,220
Yet as railroads gained prominence in moving goods and people,

105
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:42,000
St. Louis' important riverfront district

106
00:07:42,001 --> 00:07:43,500
would begin a slow decline.

107
00:07:44,460 --> 00:07:46,240
By the time the Depression hit,

108
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:48,660
the riverfront was home to one of the largest

109
00:07:48,661 --> 00:07:49,940
Hoovervilles in the country.

110
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:54,400
It once had been a bustling place when the steamboats were stopping there,

111
00:07:54,401 --> 00:07:58,580
and St. Louis was the center of river commerce for the entire American West.

112
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:00,500
But now it was in bad shape.

113
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:05,425
The neglected and abandoned buildings were also of concern to St.

114
00:08:05,426 --> 00:08:07,240
Louis lawyer, Luther Eli Smith.

115
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:13,860
Smith thought, why not destroy all these buildings, get rid of them,

116
00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:18,100
and turn this area into a real kind of garden spot?

117
00:08:18,540 --> 00:08:19,680
It wasn't a new idea.

118
00:08:19,681 --> 00:08:28,220
Other people had talked about it in the past, but Smith was the one who was well-known enough in the right circles that he could start to really push this idea.

119
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:31,158
By 1935, Smith and St.

120
00:08:31,159 --> 00:08:40,840
Louis mayor Bernard Dickman formed the Jefferson National Expansion Memorial Association to create a memorial park and a monument.

121
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:42,780
When Luther E.

122
00:08:42,781 --> 00:08:49,020
Lee Smith first pitched the idea about a memorial on the riverfront, they also wanted to weave in Thomas Jefferson.

123
00:08:49,021 --> 00:08:55,020
They felt that Jefferson was the person who really touched off American westward expansion,

124
00:08:55,380 --> 00:09:00,460
that he purchased the Louisiana Territory, that he championed the Lewis and Clark Expedition.

125
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:04,980
Smith immediately went to work raising money for the memorial.

126
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:07,860
He proved to be a shrewd fundraiser.

127
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:16,620
He got money from a particular lady, and it was a good chunk of change.

128
00:09:16,621 --> 00:09:20,140
and about a month later he went back to her,

129
00:09:20,580 --> 00:09:21,540
asked for more money.

130
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,800
And she said, hey, I just gave you money.

131
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:27,240
He said, well, you need to protect your investment.

132
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:30,200
At the height of the Depression,

133
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:33,020
Smith successfully lobbied the federal government

134
00:09:33,021 --> 00:09:35,740
to cover three-fourths of the estimated $30 million

135
00:09:35,741 --> 00:09:37,300
to build the memorial.

136
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:41,440
The city of St. Louis approved a bond to cover the rest.

137
00:09:41,441 --> 00:09:46,880
Land acquisition for the memorial park began in 1937

138
00:09:46,881 --> 00:09:52,040
But a lawsuit by 43 property owners stalled demolition

139
00:09:52,041 --> 00:09:56,140
It was a difficult road that Smith set for himself

140
00:09:56,141 --> 00:09:58,960
When he decided to make this riverfront memorial

141
00:09:58,961 --> 00:10:03,340
First of all because not everybody was exactly on his team

142
00:10:03,341 --> 00:10:05,860
Especially people who were landowners in the area

143
00:10:05,861 --> 00:10:08,800
Their property was acquired through condemnation

144
00:10:08,801 --> 00:10:15,740
and eventually all the buildings in a 40-square-block area of the city were torn down, flattened.

145
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:20,520
The landowners were bought out, and residents were forced to move elsewhere.

146
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:26,460
Further delays occurred in 1942 with the outbreak of World War II.

147
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:32,800
But as soon as it was over, Luther Eli Smith was back raising money,

148
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:36,320
this time for a nationwide architectural competition.

149
00:10:36,321 --> 00:10:40,800
And then he continued after World War II to keep the whole effort alive.

150
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:42,960
It could have easily gone by the wayside at that point.

151
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:46,200
And he personally raised the money to have a competition,

152
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:48,140
which ultimately led to the arch design.

153
00:10:49,780 --> 00:10:55,220
The competition would be one of the biggest architectural contests ever held in the United States.

154
00:10:56,160 --> 00:10:58,580
Designs would be submitted from all over the country.

155
00:11:00,340 --> 00:11:04,300
But a case of mistaken identity would keep the winning architect

156
00:11:04,301 --> 00:11:06,560
from knowing he was even in the running.

157
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:12,440
The design for the St. Louis Arch was chosen

158
00:11:12,441 --> 00:11:14,820
during one of the biggest architectural competitions

159
00:11:14,821 --> 00:11:15,960
in American history.

160
00:11:17,420 --> 00:11:21,000
The contest was launched May 30, 1947.

161
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:24,220
It was open to any architect in the United States

162
00:11:24,221 --> 00:11:27,900
and offered prizes totaling $125,000.

163
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:31,960
This was the first big competition

164
00:11:31,961 --> 00:11:33,640
that was being held anywhere in the world

165
00:11:33,641 --> 00:11:36,680
after World War II, and it's the largest amount of money

166
00:11:36,681 --> 00:11:40,380
that had ever been offered in an American architectural competition

167
00:11:40,381 --> 00:11:41,660
up to that point in time.

168
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:48,160
By the September 1st deadline, 172 entries had been submitted.

169
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:51,240
They ranged from the sublime to the bizarre,

170
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:54,980
among them pyramids, a mini Niagara Falls,

171
00:11:56,560 --> 00:11:59,940
lighthouses, and a giant compass.

172
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:03,400
A number of the designs are a little weird.

173
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:07,580
A lot of them looked a little bit like things from the World's Fairs of the 1930s.

174
00:12:07,640 --> 00:12:11,340
They're kind of streamlined and not at all like the arch in form.

175
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:16,220
The arch really was pretty much unique among the entries in being such a bold design.

176
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:20,880
The judges pared the entries down to just five

177
00:12:20,881 --> 00:12:25,080
and invited the finalists to submit drawings for a second round of competition.

178
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:30,040
Among those invited was entry number 144,

179
00:12:30,041 --> 00:12:34,380
a large stainless steel arch at the edge of a dense forest

180
00:12:34,381 --> 00:12:39,320
from the Saarinen Architectural Firm in Bloomfield Hills, Michigan.

181
00:12:41,340 --> 00:12:46,200
Finnish architect Eliel Saarinen headed the firm, along with his son, Arrow.

182
00:12:47,860 --> 00:12:50,960
Eliel Saarinen was well known around the United States.

183
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:54,280
Arrow Saarinen was his son and kind of in his shadow.

184
00:12:54,281 --> 00:12:59,600
So father and son both drew up plans for the competition.

185
00:13:00,040 --> 00:13:02,520
And they worked on opposite sides of the same wall.

186
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:06,160
And the gentleman's agreement is they didn't look at each other's designs.

187
00:13:06,580 --> 00:13:10,080
So both were submitted at the same time in the first round of the competition.

188
00:13:10,920 --> 00:13:14,280
The jury sent the winning notification off to Saarinen's home.

189
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:17,380
Everyone assumed it was for Eliel, the father.

190
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:20,200
There was three days of celebration.

191
00:13:20,760 --> 00:13:24,180
And then they sent another envoy saying,

192
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:26,740
Oh, I'm terribly sorry there's been a mistake.

193
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:29,760
It wasn't Eliel that won. It was his son.

194
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:32,040
Errol.

195
00:13:32,041 --> 00:13:37,040
Eliel Saarinen's design never made it to the final round of competition.

196
00:13:37,041 --> 00:13:41,040
And it wouldn't have been surprising in some ways if Eliel had won.

197
00:13:41,041 --> 00:13:45,040
He had done a lot of other major work at that time, but his work was more conservative.

198
00:13:45,041 --> 00:13:47,040
It was more like the work of the 1930s.

199
00:13:47,041 --> 00:13:51,040
And it was really quite a bold step for the jury to choose Errol's design.

200
00:13:51,041 --> 00:13:55,040
I think it kind of reflects this whole post-World War II sense that everything had changed

201
00:13:55,041 --> 00:14:00,820
changed and that all the ideas of the 1930s were really being put aside in favor of something new.

202
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:04,412
And the arch is a very clear expression of that.

203
00:14:04,413 --> 00:14:07,160
No other design came close to Eero Saarinen's

204
00:14:07,161 --> 00:14:08,165
stunning vision.

205
00:14:08,166 --> 00:14:12,940
He had found a way to create something entirely new by looking back into the

206
00:14:12,941 --> 00:14:17,612
past. The basic idea was to create a very simple, clear geometric form.

207
00:14:17,613 --> 00:14:19,520
He looked at the presidential

208
00:14:19,521 --> 00:14:23,969
monuments in Washington. The Lincoln Memorial, a simple rectangle.

209
00:14:23,970 --> 00:14:25,520
The Jefferson Memorial,

210
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:26,500
a sphere.

211
00:14:26,501 --> 00:14:31,200
He first experimented actually with a dome shape for the monument, but then realized

212
00:14:31,201 --> 00:14:34,377
that that was probably too backward looking, too neoclassical.

213
00:14:34,378 --> 00:14:36,120
And then from there, kind of moved

214
00:14:36,121 --> 00:14:37,722
to the idea of the arch.

215
00:14:37,723 --> 00:14:41,860
Saarinen's striking catenary curve in a stainless steel sheath

216
00:14:41,861 --> 00:14:44,730
was his modern homage to the classical arch.

217
00:14:44,731 --> 00:14:47,860
It was also the perfect expression of a gateway.

218
00:14:47,861 --> 00:14:54,180
It's an incredible structure and you can tell that Saarinen was not only an architect,

219
00:14:54,181 --> 00:14:56,540
but he studied sculpture.

220
00:14:56,541 --> 00:15:03,520
He wanted it to be beautiful from any angle and so he was just very careful and very meticulous

221
00:15:03,521 --> 00:15:11,460
and almost to the point of obsession in getting the angle just right so that we get the effect

222
00:15:11,461 --> 00:15:13,840
that we have today.

223
00:15:13,841 --> 00:15:16,280
The judges of the architectural competition were delighted,

224
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:19,260
but initial reaction from the public was mixed.

225
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:22,900
There were some people who just said,

226
00:15:23,340 --> 00:15:24,960
you know, it looks like a big croquet wicket,

227
00:15:25,380 --> 00:15:29,220
or, you know, they'll never be able to really build this thing.

228
00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:31,240
You know, there was a lot of question just about

229
00:15:31,241 --> 00:15:33,220
whether it could actually be done or not.

230
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:37,820
However, the most serious criticism came from a fellow architect.

231
00:15:38,380 --> 00:15:41,200
In 1948, right after the design was announced,

232
00:15:41,620 --> 00:15:45,120
Gilmore Clark, who was a landscape architect in New York,

233
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:49,240
charged that the design was basically a copy of a fascist design

234
00:15:49,241 --> 00:15:54,680
for a university and exposition that Mussolini had sponsored in 1942 outside of Rome.

235
00:15:56,760 --> 00:16:01,840
Newspapers picked up the story and printed pictures of Saarinen's arch next to Mussolini's arch.

236
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:04,560
The arches looked identical.

237
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:07,960
Saarinen thought the charge preposterous.

238
00:16:07,961 --> 00:16:14,360
He had never heard of Mussolini's arch and stated that an arch, any arch, has no ideological connections.

239
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:20,640
His biggest offender was the Jefferson National Expansion Memorial Association.

240
00:16:21,340 --> 00:16:23,500
It brought to light what most had overlooked.

241
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:28,900
Mussolini's arch was never built, and it was different, shorter and wider.

242
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:33,180
The association went on to endorse Saarinen's arch as, quote,

243
00:16:33,181 --> 00:16:35,060
a beautiful and inspiring form,

244
00:16:35,820 --> 00:16:38,400
perfect in its symbolism as a gateway to the West.

245
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:41,400
The controversy soon died,

246
00:16:41,980 --> 00:16:44,280
and Sarening quickly plunged into the work needed

247
00:16:44,281 --> 00:16:45,580
to make the arch a reality.

248
00:16:47,260 --> 00:16:49,500
But as design on the arch progressed,

249
00:16:50,160 --> 00:16:52,140
the Jefferson National Expansion Memorial

250
00:16:52,141 --> 00:16:53,640
lost its greatest champion,

251
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:56,720
Luther Eli Smith.

252
00:16:57,360 --> 00:16:59,240
He died in 1951,

253
00:16:59,660 --> 00:17:01,220
but his dream lived on.

254
00:17:01,221 --> 00:17:10,200
My father took over Jefferson National Expansion Memorial Association when my grandfather died.

255
00:17:10,201 --> 00:17:15,600
And all I heard around the house was the arch, and we have little arches everywhere.

256
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:17,780
I mean, the big ones, little ones, plates.

257
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:22,440
I think everybody was crazy about the design.

258
00:17:22,820 --> 00:17:24,300
It was really amazing.

259
00:17:25,300 --> 00:17:29,560
By 1959, the last of federal funding was in place,

260
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,760
and construction on the St. Louis Arch could finally begin.

261
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:40,280
In 1961, however, Arrow Saarinen was diagnosed with a brain tumor.

262
00:17:41,180 --> 00:17:45,620
He died soon after at the age of 51, never seeing the arch completed.

263
00:17:46,740 --> 00:17:53,900
If Arrow were to see the arch today, I think he would look at it dispassionately.

264
00:17:54,300 --> 00:17:59,300
and wonder had he done everything just the way it ought to be done.

265
00:17:59,301 --> 00:18:05,300
And it's only now in retrospect, looking back, that we can see that in 1948,

266
00:18:05,301 --> 00:18:19,300
this very clear, elegant, engineeringly beautiful solution was absolutely perfect for its moment in time.

267
00:18:19,301 --> 00:18:27,520
I think he was a great architect and great sculptor and sort of a renaissance man.

268
00:18:27,780 --> 00:18:34,660
He was clever with form, but he was also clever with ideas, with concept.

269
00:18:35,780 --> 00:18:41,560
And that's a hard combination to find somebody that's like that.

270
00:18:41,561 --> 00:18:48,840
Aero Saarinen and Luther Eli Smith, the two most important people responsible for the

271
00:18:48,841 --> 00:18:54,382
Gateway Arch, were now gone. But the arch still had to be built.

272
00:18:54,383 --> 00:18:56,200
Essentially, it would

273
00:18:56,201 --> 00:19:02,466
be two elongated towers consisting of over 17,000 tons of steel and concrete.

274
00:19:02,467 --> 00:19:03,280
The towers

275
00:19:03,281 --> 00:19:10,474
would have to curve in the sky, meet in the middle, and not snap in high winds.

276
00:19:10,475 --> 00:19:11,175
It was

277
00:19:11,176 --> 00:19:15,710
chief designer John Dinkaloo, who devised a way to anchor each leg.

278
00:19:15,711 --> 00:19:17,600
He suggested an orthotropic

279
00:19:17,601 --> 00:19:20,680
design, the first of its kind in the United States.

280
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:27,260
Orthotropic design that was used on the Gateway Arch basically does not involve a skeleton,

281
00:19:27,520 --> 00:19:32,322
a steel skeleton, the usual structure that you'd see, a skyscraper.

282
00:19:32,323 --> 00:19:33,540
First you see the

283
00:19:33,541 --> 00:19:39,380
steel skeleton rising, and then they put the elements on the outside of it, the windows

284
00:19:39,381 --> 00:19:42,380
or the solid walls or whatever it is.

285
00:19:42,900 --> 00:19:47,340
And with the gateway arch, what you see is what's actually holding the structure up.

286
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:52,540
The exterior walls of stainless steel and interior walls of carbon steel

287
00:19:52,541 --> 00:19:56,020
with concrete pressed between them carry the structural load.

288
00:19:56,760 --> 00:20:00,200
This design allows the thrust to pass through the legs

289
00:20:00,201 --> 00:20:02,080
and be absorbed in the foundations.

290
00:20:02,081 --> 00:20:09,560
For the base of the legs, 300,000 feet of earth and rock were excavated.

291
00:20:11,100 --> 00:20:15,920
Footings 60 feet deep were sunk, 30 feet of that into limestone bedrock.

292
00:20:16,780 --> 00:20:20,380
Nearly 26,000 tons of concrete were poured into these footings.

293
00:20:21,660 --> 00:20:23,700
It was quite an extensive concrete pour.

294
00:20:23,900 --> 00:20:28,460
I guess you could say it would be the same as like a six-story building underground,

295
00:20:28,461 --> 00:20:33,020
that's solid concrete that supports the legs of the arch

296
00:20:33,021 --> 00:20:34,280
on each side.

297
00:20:35,300 --> 00:20:37,440
To anchor the legs firmly to this base,

298
00:20:38,020 --> 00:20:41,120
the concrete was laid down in four 11-foot layers,

299
00:20:41,620 --> 00:20:43,960
laced with networks of steel tensioning rods,

300
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:46,220
252 to a leg.

301
00:20:47,780 --> 00:20:50,920
Buried into the actual foundations,

302
00:20:51,120 --> 00:20:52,160
the concrete structure,

303
00:20:52,161 --> 00:20:54,760
were pre-stressing rods,

304
00:20:56,280 --> 00:20:57,840
post-tensioning rods,

305
00:20:57,841 --> 00:21:03,240
that would actually be extended as the arch was built.

306
00:21:03,360 --> 00:21:04,660
They'd keep adding to them.

307
00:21:05,040 --> 00:21:10,080
And those would go up the back of the leg on each side of the arch,

308
00:21:10,120 --> 00:21:12,880
almost like the tendons in your own leg.

309
00:21:12,960 --> 00:21:19,080
And those would hold the leg back and keep it as erect as possible as it was going up.

310
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:23,480
Construction of the St. Louis Arch could now begin.

311
00:21:24,260 --> 00:21:26,560
It would be a dangerous and unfamiliar task.

312
00:21:26,561 --> 00:21:31,900
two major challenges were how to carry the tons of steel to the top and how to

313
00:21:31,901 --> 00:21:36,340
provide a work area for crew members without traditional scaffolding.

314
00:21:36,341 --> 00:21:41,680
The solution was the creation of a piece of construction equipment the world had

315
00:21:41,681 --> 00:21:49,460
never seen before. February 1963, with the massive foundations in place,

316
00:21:49,461 --> 00:21:54,520
construction on the arch itself could now begin. Nothing like this had ever been

317
00:21:54,521 --> 00:21:57,250
done before. It was completely unique.

318
00:21:57,251 --> 00:22:01,560
Construction workers faced frigid winter days and sweltering

319
00:22:01,561 --> 00:22:02,261
summer heat.

320
00:22:02,262 --> 00:22:06,700
But they would face dangers far more significant than those posed by the climate.

321
00:22:08,080 --> 00:22:14,760
All construction is dangerous, but this particular project, since it was unique and no one working

322
00:22:14,761 --> 00:22:21,200
on it really had any experience of a similar kind of project, made it doubly dangerous.

323
00:22:21,201 --> 00:22:31,420
In the days before strict safety laws, workers would be on steel beams with no support, hundreds of feet in the air or dangling in high winds.

324
00:22:32,140 --> 00:22:39,040
I personally don't think that the arch could be built today because of OSHA restrictions and other things.

325
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:43,840
I wonder whether it ever could soar to 630 feet the way that it did.

326
00:22:43,841 --> 00:22:49,780
You see men up there in the photographs and in the film where they're just hanging on,

327
00:22:49,920 --> 00:22:53,080
and sometimes the winds would get high during a storm or something,

328
00:22:53,260 --> 00:22:57,660
and just sort of clinging to the sides of this thing, you know, while it's shaking around.

329
00:22:57,980 --> 00:23:02,720
And these guys, you see them up there, they didn't even have safety harnesses on or anything else.

330
00:23:03,400 --> 00:23:08,900
The insurance company underwriting the project predicted that 13 people would die during the building of the arch.

331
00:23:09,800 --> 00:23:11,480
Nonetheless, construction proceeded.

332
00:23:11,481 --> 00:23:17,480
The basic building unit of the arch was the double-walled triangular section.

333
00:23:18,240 --> 00:23:22,940
It weighed over 50 tons and was hollow to allow room for a tram system.

334
00:23:24,220 --> 00:23:27,560
142 of these building blocks would be needed to complete the arch.

335
00:23:28,660 --> 00:23:31,780
Each unit would consist of six stainless steel panels.

336
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:38,700
Those six panels of stainless were welded together into one side of the lower sections

337
00:23:38,701 --> 00:23:42,480
in the fabricating plants in Pennsylvania,

338
00:23:43,320 --> 00:23:46,700
and then they were joined with the interior surface.

339
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:49,980
The Pittsburgh Des Moines Steel Company,

340
00:23:50,160 --> 00:23:53,360
which had specialized in building bridges and elevator water towers,

341
00:23:53,880 --> 00:23:58,000
was responsible for the nearly 1,000 tons of polished stainless steel.

342
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,600
The sections were transported by rail from steel mills in Pittsburgh.

343
00:24:03,420 --> 00:24:08,320
At the job site, the triangular wall sections were assembled on a concrete welding pad.

344
00:24:08,700 --> 00:24:20,080
They would assemble them into the triangular shape, weld the corners, and then they were transferred by a railroad car, a rebuilt railroad car, under the arch legs.

345
00:24:21,300 --> 00:24:24,320
Lifting an arch section into place took only half an hour.

346
00:24:25,020 --> 00:24:29,860
They were connected to each other by high-strength steel tensioning rods and then welded together.

347
00:24:31,140 --> 00:24:34,420
They would set it on top of the previously set section.

348
00:24:34,421 --> 00:24:39,980
These were then lowered down, and there were backup bars.

349
00:24:40,400 --> 00:24:45,060
By backup bars, I mean steel bars that were on the back side

350
00:24:45,061 --> 00:24:47,020
because you could only weld from one side.

351
00:24:48,080 --> 00:24:50,840
The legs of the arch would be built simultaneously.

352
00:24:51,640 --> 00:24:54,960
Because the arch was to be so tall, one of the major problems

353
00:24:54,961 --> 00:24:58,200
was determining how to raise the steel sections into place.

354
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:02,680
The first six sections of each leg, 72 feet,

355
00:25:02,681 --> 00:25:04,780
were handled with conventional cranes.

356
00:25:05,740 --> 00:25:08,800
Beyond that, it was necessary to use something completely different.

357
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:15,240
There were no cranes that were going to be able to lift pieces into place

358
00:25:15,241 --> 00:25:21,100
that were tall enough to get up above 60, 100 feet.

359
00:25:21,660 --> 00:25:24,580
So they had to think of something, another way to do it.

360
00:25:24,980 --> 00:25:29,120
The answer was the creeper derrick, specially designed for this project.

361
00:25:29,121 --> 00:25:34,700
Each of the two 80-ton assemblies was essentially a tiltable platform mounted

362
00:25:34,701 --> 00:25:39,580
on railroad tracks that were actually placed on sides of the legs. On top of

363
00:25:39,581 --> 00:25:46,080
each platform was a 130-foot crane. The design for these creepers where we had

364
00:25:46,081 --> 00:25:52,280
to change the elevation of the platform as the construction progressed was a

365
00:25:52,281 --> 00:25:58,880
little different and required some very complete engineering to make sure that

366
00:25:58,881 --> 00:26:01,740
it would work in all situations on the arch.

367
00:26:02,520 --> 00:26:05,020
The rigs climbed to predetermined stations,

368
00:26:05,500 --> 00:26:07,200
and the cranes then lifted the sections

369
00:26:07,201 --> 00:26:10,320
as well as the reinforcing steel and post-tensioning bars.

370
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:15,600
As the derricks pulled themselves up the curved legs of the arch,

371
00:26:16,660 --> 00:26:18,640
adjustable supports kept them level

372
00:26:18,641 --> 00:26:20,740
regardless of the curvature of the legs.

373
00:26:22,420 --> 00:26:24,780
Rail was added every 48 feet,

374
00:26:26,040 --> 00:26:28,360
or after four sections were installed.

375
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:30,980
workers would then move the derrick up again.

376
00:26:32,200 --> 00:26:36,020
As the work climbed higher and higher

377
00:26:36,021 --> 00:26:38,220
and the arch started to bend over,

378
00:26:38,780 --> 00:26:42,700
the platform had to keep going at more and more of an acute angle

379
00:26:42,701 --> 00:26:45,800
from the place where it was attached onto the tracks.

380
00:26:46,540 --> 00:26:48,800
All of the methods of construction

381
00:26:48,801 --> 00:26:54,580
were, in a sense, first-time unique methods.

382
00:26:55,860 --> 00:26:58,100
The creeper derricks were part of that.

383
00:26:58,101 --> 00:27:07,860
The idea of putting a rail line up for the derricks to be able to climb up on the arch as it was being constructed was a very, very original idea.

384
00:27:08,640 --> 00:27:14,620
Since the arch legs were being built at exactly the same time, sophisticated surveying was required.

385
00:27:15,800 --> 00:27:23,440
Measurements had to be very precise, since a slight deviation in any direction could result in the legs failing to meet perfectly at the top.

386
00:27:23,441 --> 00:27:29,920
To eliminate heat distortion caused by the sun's rays striking one side of the arch while

387
00:27:29,921 --> 00:27:34,183
the other side was in shadow, all measurements were done at night.

388
00:27:34,184 --> 00:27:35,540
Accuracy was achieved

389
00:27:35,541 --> 00:27:36,947
through triangulation.

390
00:27:36,948 --> 00:27:41,040
Engineers used four posts, or monuments, that formed a rectangle

391
00:27:41,041 --> 00:27:45,040
300 by 720 feet centered on the arch legs.

392
00:27:45,041 --> 00:27:50,780
We established four monuments, two behind each leg of the arch and majored the distance

393
00:27:50,781 --> 00:27:52,900
between those two monuments very accurately.

394
00:27:53,960 --> 00:27:56,760
Surveyors employed an instrument called a theodolite,

395
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,200
a device similar to a telescope

396
00:27:59,201 --> 00:28:01,900
that measures horizontal and vertical angles.

397
00:28:03,580 --> 00:28:05,540
Engineers used the two north monuments

398
00:28:05,541 --> 00:28:07,680
for triangulation on the south leg

399
00:28:07,681 --> 00:28:11,260
and the two south monuments for triangulation on the north leg.

400
00:28:12,180 --> 00:28:14,020
And by applying the rules of trigonometry,

401
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:16,580
the engineers could precisely determine

402
00:28:16,581 --> 00:28:18,740
any given point along the arch legs.

403
00:28:19,740 --> 00:28:23,640
Accuracy had to be maintained to a 64th of an inch.

404
00:28:25,580 --> 00:28:30,040
Thousands of tons of steel moved closer and closer to the arch's apex.

405
00:28:30,580 --> 00:28:35,100
But before Luther Smith's dream and Eero Saarinen's work of art could be completed,

406
00:28:35,620 --> 00:28:38,500
more technical challenges would have to be overcome.

407
00:28:41,620 --> 00:28:45,340
After two and a half years of building St. Louis' Gateway Arch,

408
00:28:45,900 --> 00:28:50,200
construction was on schedule for an October 1965 completion date.

409
00:28:50,960 --> 00:28:54,700
As the two freestanding arch legs reached 530 feet,

410
00:28:55,380 --> 00:28:58,660
engineers faced the major concern that large construction loads

411
00:28:58,661 --> 00:29:01,840
and the weight of the derricks on top would bend the legs.

412
00:29:02,700 --> 00:29:05,160
The legs were rocking and rolling up there.

413
00:29:05,280 --> 00:29:06,100
They were moving around.

414
00:29:06,220 --> 00:29:07,300
The wind was blowing everything.

415
00:29:07,800 --> 00:29:10,200
And you had the problem of the weight,

416
00:29:10,201 --> 00:29:14,480
and the weight was starting to make the legs start to topple inward.

417
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:17,480
One of the big questions they had, as I understand it,

418
00:29:17,481 --> 00:29:21,880
is how do we keep these two sides stable

419
00:29:21,881 --> 00:29:25,620
until we can get the final section in and the weight can be released?

420
00:29:26,260 --> 00:29:30,040
The solution was a 60-ton stabilizing truss,

421
00:29:30,320 --> 00:29:35,560
which would be installed horizontally between the two legs on June 17, 1965.

422
00:29:37,680 --> 00:29:40,320
This would be one of the most challenging tasks,

423
00:29:40,580 --> 00:29:44,100
as the two creeper derrick operators would meticulously work together

424
00:29:44,101 --> 00:29:45,100
position the truss.

425
00:29:48,580 --> 00:29:51,740
Any two crane lift is kind of a dangerous situation

426
00:29:51,840 --> 00:29:52,840
to start with.

427
00:29:52,940 --> 00:29:55,940
They have to keep it level, otherwise the load

428
00:29:56,040 --> 00:29:58,680
transfers to the other one.

429
00:29:58,780 --> 00:30:04,080
They tied a rope on each end of it, and they, before they,

430
00:30:04,180 --> 00:30:06,960
the night before, they marked it off in increments of 10 feet

431
00:30:07,060 --> 00:30:11,260
or so, so when the rope went through the pulleys that they

432
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:13,940
had mounted on the ground, well, it was just like a

433
00:30:13,941 --> 00:30:15,940
measuring tape going up.

434
00:30:15,941 --> 00:30:22,280
They had to do the most complicated lift of the whole project where both

435
00:30:22,281 --> 00:30:27,260
creeper derrick operators had to be working in exact synchronicity with one

436
00:30:27,261 --> 00:30:33,060
another to pull this thing up. So you had a creeper derrick operator on the

437
00:30:33,061 --> 00:30:37,580
ground on the south side, one on the ground on the north side, both of them

438
00:30:37,581 --> 00:30:41,420
listening to commands of two guys with walkie-talkies at the top of the arch.

439
00:30:41,421 --> 00:30:43,920
OK, Bill, our big load's even with the truck.

440
00:30:43,921 --> 00:30:46,220
All right, now, Bill, we're going to get our quake.

441
00:30:46,221 --> 00:30:47,920
We're going to get our quake.

442
00:30:47,921 --> 00:30:48,820
Hold that, Bill.

443
00:30:48,821 --> 00:30:49,920
Hold that.

444
00:30:49,921 --> 00:30:53,960
And they're both raising this huge, like,

445
00:30:53,961 --> 00:30:58,340
60-ton piece of steel that's almost as long as a football

446
00:30:58,341 --> 00:30:59,600
field, you know.

447
00:30:59,601 --> 00:31:02,700
And they're raising this thing, the two sides slowly up

448
00:31:02,701 --> 00:31:03,401
into place.

449
00:31:03,402 --> 00:31:05,880
And they don't want to get one side too far ahead of the other.

450
00:31:05,881 --> 00:31:08,240
So when they get it to the top, they can lock it in.

451
00:31:08,241 --> 00:31:16,440
after the stabilizing truss was in place 21 additional triangular sections were added to

452
00:31:16,441 --> 00:31:22,360
each leg the next step would be to put the final keystone section into place to complete the arch

453
00:31:22,361 --> 00:31:28,940
the keystone section allows all the loads of the arch to transfer directly to the foundations

454
00:31:28,941 --> 00:31:36,860
it's all based on the the classic concept that goes back to the romans of of an arch and the way

455
00:31:36,861 --> 00:31:38,760
that the weight of an arch is supported,

456
00:31:39,220 --> 00:31:43,000
and until a keystone is put in place to absorb the weight,

457
00:31:43,700 --> 00:31:46,920
and so the two sides push in on one another

458
00:31:46,921 --> 00:31:48,620
and hold each other up.

459
00:31:49,580 --> 00:31:52,160
The St. Louis mayor wanted the last piece

460
00:31:52,161 --> 00:31:53,520
put in on a Saturday morning,

461
00:31:54,120 --> 00:31:56,800
but construction officials felt a greater sense of urgency

462
00:31:56,801 --> 00:31:57,840
to complete the job.

463
00:31:58,840 --> 00:32:02,700
We had approximately 100 feet of stainless steel

464
00:32:02,701 --> 00:32:03,880
sticking above that.

465
00:32:04,460 --> 00:32:06,560
It's got an 80-ton derrick sitting on top of it.

466
00:32:06,561 --> 00:32:11,400
If we'd had an extremely tough windstorm or something like that,

467
00:32:11,940 --> 00:32:14,400
let's face it, it's 600 feet in the air.

468
00:32:14,660 --> 00:32:17,500
The arch is in a very vulnerable position.

469
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:23,660
The completion date was moved up two days to Thursday, October 28, 1965.

470
00:32:25,140 --> 00:32:27,640
Anticipation in St. Louis was running high.

471
00:32:28,340 --> 00:32:31,200
For two and a half years, people had watched this thing being built,

472
00:32:31,320 --> 00:32:34,620
you know, piece by piece and gradually rising above the horizon.

473
00:32:34,621 --> 00:32:37,720
and soon you have this beautiful, gigantic thing,

474
00:32:37,721 --> 00:32:40,600
but everybody's question was, is it going to meet?

475
00:32:40,601 --> 00:32:42,760
There were even cartoons that existed at the time

476
00:32:42,761 --> 00:32:45,440
that showed that the two legs missed one another

477
00:32:45,441 --> 00:32:48,800
and they were kind of still building and going past.

478
00:32:48,801 --> 00:32:53,140
So they showed it in schools, on television,

479
00:32:53,141 --> 00:32:55,180
closed circuit TV and all kinds of things,

480
00:32:55,181 --> 00:32:56,980
and there were a lot of people who took off work

481
00:32:56,981 --> 00:32:59,740
and were down waiting for this big moment

482
00:32:59,741 --> 00:33:01,180
of joining of the two legs.

483
00:33:01,181 --> 00:33:09,620
On a bright sunny day, as 10,000 people watched anxiously from the ground, the final piece was to be put in place.

484
00:33:10,660 --> 00:33:14,300
However, the sun on the south leg of the arch was causing it to expand.

485
00:33:14,820 --> 00:33:17,520
The leg itself would start to warp, if you will.

486
00:33:17,940 --> 00:33:19,460
And so it would warp out of position.

487
00:33:20,020 --> 00:33:24,220
And once that happened, especially by the time it got up 630 feet into the air,

488
00:33:24,540 --> 00:33:28,500
there was no way you were going to be able to sort of skew it back into the right position.

489
00:33:28,501 --> 00:33:32,500
So what they did is they actually got the fire department,

490
00:33:32,501 --> 00:33:34,500
St. Louis Fire Department, to come out with their hoses

491
00:33:34,501 --> 00:33:39,500
and wash the leg with cold water up to quite a height,

492
00:33:39,501 --> 00:33:42,500
I'd say 200, 250 feet.

493
00:33:42,501 --> 00:33:45,500
And the orcs look like Niagara Falls, really,

494
00:33:45,501 --> 00:33:48,500
with all the water coming down there.

495
00:33:48,501 --> 00:33:51,500
The cold water tempered the expansion on the south leg,

496
00:33:51,501 --> 00:33:54,500
but the intense sun still caused the legs to lean

497
00:33:54,501 --> 00:33:57,500
to within 2 1⁄2 feet of each other.

498
00:33:57,501 --> 00:34:00,880
The keystone was 8 1⁄2 feet long.

499
00:34:01,600 --> 00:34:04,540
Fortunately, engineers had anticipated the problem

500
00:34:04,541 --> 00:34:08,400
and had hydraulic jacks that could apply a million pounds of thrust

501
00:34:08,401 --> 00:34:09,580
to widen the gap.

502
00:34:09,940 --> 00:34:12,540
That would allow the last section to be inserted.

503
00:34:13,580 --> 00:34:15,940
Ironically, the man performing that task,

504
00:34:16,120 --> 00:34:18,240
unlike the throng witnessing this event,

505
00:34:18,760 --> 00:34:21,520
would not actually see the keystone going into place.

506
00:34:23,680 --> 00:34:26,600
I was probably, probably was the only person

507
00:34:26,601 --> 00:34:31,720
and then the whole country, or I don't know if we can say the world or not,

508
00:34:31,800 --> 00:34:34,720
but I was probably the only one that had never seen it go into place

509
00:34:34,721 --> 00:34:40,560
because the operator of the derricks were in a shack

510
00:34:40,561 --> 00:34:43,500
100 foot behind the leg of the arch.

511
00:34:44,280 --> 00:34:48,520
He had to listen to very specific commands that were given to him

512
00:34:48,521 --> 00:34:51,940
over like a walkie-talkie or telephone type of thing, intercom,

513
00:34:51,941 --> 00:34:55,120
which, you know, two inches to the right,

514
00:34:55,200 --> 00:34:56,700
two inches to the left type of thing

515
00:34:56,701 --> 00:34:57,980
to put the piece in place.

516
00:34:58,820 --> 00:35:00,940
Vito was my phone operator,

517
00:35:01,160 --> 00:35:03,900
or really my eyes in the sky, so to speak.

518
00:35:04,520 --> 00:35:06,820
He was the man that directed the crane

519
00:35:06,821 --> 00:35:09,180
as to get up a little bit higher

520
00:35:09,181 --> 00:35:11,060
or swing right or swing left

521
00:35:11,061 --> 00:35:14,300
or to boom up or down, whatever was needed.

522
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:17,120
As the crowd cheered,

523
00:35:17,520 --> 00:35:20,040
the final piece slipped evenly into place.

524
00:35:20,660 --> 00:35:22,080
The jack pressure was released.

525
00:35:22,640 --> 00:35:25,660
The natural thrust of the legs clamped the section into place,

526
00:35:25,960 --> 00:35:27,100
and it was welded together.

527
00:35:27,960 --> 00:35:30,860
It was such an important date in St. Louis history

528
00:35:30,861 --> 00:35:34,780
is probably because people have been waiting so long for this thing to happen.

529
00:35:35,460 --> 00:35:38,700
At no time during the construction of the arch did we ever,

530
00:35:38,920 --> 00:35:41,160
anyone that was closely associated with the project,

531
00:35:41,460 --> 00:35:43,860
did we ever feel that this thing would not meet at the top.

532
00:35:44,500 --> 00:35:47,380
They built it up on each side symmetrically

533
00:35:47,381 --> 00:35:50,420
so that both halves of the arch went up at the same time.

534
00:35:50,740 --> 00:35:53,100
Incredible level of precision in the construction.

535
00:35:53,360 --> 00:35:54,060
It is amazing.

536
00:35:55,320 --> 00:35:57,740
Now that the outside of the arch was completed,

537
00:35:58,460 --> 00:36:00,560
engineers turned to the next big project,

538
00:36:01,040 --> 00:36:05,180
installing a tram system that would take hundreds of thousands of visitors a year

539
00:36:05,181 --> 00:36:07,380
to the top of the curved arch and back.

540
00:36:08,420 --> 00:36:10,640
But how to design this unique system

541
00:36:10,641 --> 00:36:14,160
would stymie the top elevator experts in the world.

542
00:36:17,380 --> 00:36:22,380
The magnificent exterior of the St. Louis Arch was now complete.

543
00:36:22,381 --> 00:36:27,380
The next major task was building the unique tram system, one that would transport visitors

544
00:36:27,381 --> 00:36:31,380
up the tallest memorial in the United States.

545
00:36:31,381 --> 00:36:36,380
Construction began shortly after completion of the outside of the arch.

546
00:36:36,381 --> 00:36:42,380
However, the search to find someone to design a passenger system that would travel up and down a curved arch

547
00:36:42,381 --> 00:36:46,380
began five years earlier in 1960.

548
00:36:46,381 --> 00:36:52,040
From his Detroit office, arch-architect Aero Saarinen approached the top elevator experts in the world,

549
00:36:52,360 --> 00:36:54,300
but none of them could come up with a solution.

550
00:36:56,240 --> 00:37:05,560
Well, the unique challenges in developing this system, of course,

551
00:37:05,561 --> 00:37:11,520
were it's the level of precision which had to be followed in order to make it work,

552
00:37:11,521 --> 00:37:17,140
Going up to 630 feet in a diminishing triangular space.

553
00:37:17,740 --> 00:37:21,000
Then, an accidental meeting turned to good fortune.

554
00:37:22,300 --> 00:37:25,300
It was just pure luck that a guy named Dick Bowser

555
00:37:25,301 --> 00:37:29,240
walked into the offices of Montgomery Elevator Company one day,

556
00:37:29,340 --> 00:37:32,580
just happenstance, came by to see a friend,

557
00:37:33,120 --> 00:37:35,620
and the friend had been talking with the Saarinen people

558
00:37:35,621 --> 00:37:38,600
and, you know, started a... the light bulb went off in his head

559
00:37:38,601 --> 00:37:42,200
and he said, you know, this guy, this guy's really inventive.

560
00:37:42,260 --> 00:37:44,940
He might be able to be the one that could solve the problem for them.

561
00:37:46,420 --> 00:37:48,880
Dick Bowser was a college dropout.

562
00:37:49,560 --> 00:37:53,800
He and his father had developed and installed Bowser Parking System elevator equipment.

563
00:37:55,980 --> 00:38:00,220
The elevators traveled horizontally and vertically as well as diagonally.

564
00:38:02,380 --> 00:38:06,960
Saarinen was familiar with the system and immediately hired Bowser in June of 1960.

565
00:38:07,920 --> 00:38:10,200
While Saranen wholeheartedly endorsed Bowser,

566
00:38:11,020 --> 00:38:12,860
city officials questioned his background.

567
00:38:13,900 --> 00:38:15,860
Hoping to hire someone with more experience,

568
00:38:16,340 --> 00:38:18,180
they gave him an impossible deadline,

569
00:38:18,680 --> 00:38:20,820
deliver a proposal in two weeks.

570
00:38:22,580 --> 00:38:25,040
I'll tell you the truth, my reaction was,

571
00:38:25,041 --> 00:38:27,660
they're going to pay me for that two weeks.

572
00:38:28,180 --> 00:38:31,740
The National Park Service, as the agency overseeing the arch,

573
00:38:31,860 --> 00:38:33,060
required two things.

574
00:38:33,061 --> 00:38:37,080
a passenger volume of 3,500 people in an eight-hour day,

575
00:38:37,640 --> 00:38:41,600
and a conveyance system that would not distort the exterior of the arch.

576
00:38:43,700 --> 00:38:46,060
Bowser first considered a standard elevator,

577
00:38:46,560 --> 00:38:49,600
but putting a square box in a curved arch was not practical.

578
00:38:50,980 --> 00:38:52,800
Bowser also thought about the escalator.

579
00:38:53,600 --> 00:38:57,220
An escalator running at an angle in that curved arch,

580
00:38:57,800 --> 00:39:00,320
it just got too involved.

581
00:39:00,321 --> 00:39:05,920
and you'd had to change escalators every 20 or 30 feet up there.

582
00:39:06,600 --> 00:39:09,680
He then considered the Ferris wheel as a possible model.

583
00:39:10,140 --> 00:39:13,700
The problem was the distance up one leg and down the other and back

584
00:39:13,701 --> 00:39:15,200
was over half a mile,

585
00:39:15,640 --> 00:39:19,020
too unrealistic at that time for any chain or cable to travel.

586
00:39:19,480 --> 00:39:23,800
It had to go on slowly and it would never stop.

587
00:39:23,880 --> 00:39:25,200
It just had to keep going.

588
00:39:26,040 --> 00:39:29,540
He started to get the idea that he had to have two independent systems,

589
00:39:29,541 --> 00:39:30,660
one for each leg.

590
00:39:31,400 --> 00:39:33,260
Bowser finally came up with a solution

591
00:39:33,261 --> 00:39:35,280
combining the principle of the Ferris wheel

592
00:39:35,281 --> 00:39:36,500
with that of the elevator.

593
00:39:38,020 --> 00:39:42,240
I thought about the barrels for people to ride inside of it,

594
00:39:42,720 --> 00:39:45,000
and then it was independently powered.

595
00:39:45,560 --> 00:39:51,020
Each one had a hoist machine and counterweights and everything,

596
00:39:51,780 --> 00:39:54,860
and it only took half of the arch sections.

597
00:39:56,020 --> 00:39:59,180
The tram elements, the tracks and everything,

598
00:39:59,181 --> 00:40:01,440
were put into place while the arch was being built,

599
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:04,860
but then the cars had to be fabricated.

600
00:40:05,620 --> 00:40:09,100
The idea for the car capsules came from a magazine ad.

601
00:40:09,960 --> 00:40:12,180
And it was a new automobile at that time,

602
00:40:12,260 --> 00:40:14,640
a compact automobile called the Ford Falcon,

603
00:40:15,220 --> 00:40:17,220
and they actually had a little inset

604
00:40:17,221 --> 00:40:19,300
of how the seats were arranged in there,

605
00:40:19,680 --> 00:40:21,840
and he took that and actually used that

606
00:40:21,841 --> 00:40:27,020
as a kind of a model to show how a small space

607
00:40:27,021 --> 00:40:29,760
could accommodate several people up to five.

608
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:33,760
From this idea, he developed his final design.

609
00:40:34,520 --> 00:40:37,960
It was an eight-capsule train powered by an elevator mechanism.

610
00:40:39,520 --> 00:40:40,860
Inside are five seats.

611
00:40:41,320 --> 00:40:44,240
The capsules are similar to the barrels used in a cement mixer.

612
00:40:46,180 --> 00:40:48,780
There's a pivot in the back of the capsule

613
00:40:48,781 --> 00:40:53,380
and the door on the front, and it's just open in there.

614
00:40:53,381 --> 00:41:04,180
and that's why it's like a cement mixer because it has a pivot point and then the outside is a ring with wheels on it.

615
00:41:05,180 --> 00:41:10,220
The barrel can rotate within the frame that is supported by wheels running in the channel-shaped tracks.

616
00:41:11,280 --> 00:41:19,000
When the train is in the lower load zone, it actually hangs below the track.

617
00:41:19,001 --> 00:41:22,100
and then as it goes up the arch,

618
00:41:22,440 --> 00:41:25,100
it gradually gets over to when it gets up the top,

619
00:41:25,180 --> 00:41:26,840
it's sitting on top of the track.

620
00:41:28,480 --> 00:41:31,700
Even today, there is no other system like it in the world.

621
00:41:32,240 --> 00:41:34,540
Almost a million people a year ride the tram

622
00:41:34,541 --> 00:41:36,560
for spectacular views at the top,

623
00:41:37,060 --> 00:41:40,740
but few were aware of the risks it took to build the arch.

624
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:45,040
Despite the insurance company's dire prediction

625
00:41:45,041 --> 00:41:47,700
that 13 people would perish during the construction,

626
00:41:47,701 --> 00:41:49,700
Not one worker died.

627
00:41:49,701 --> 00:42:01,700
I think that everybody on the project realized that safety was first and foremost on this job.

628
00:42:01,701 --> 00:42:04,700
We had an excellent safety record.

629
00:42:04,701 --> 00:42:09,700
At one time we had over 500 working days without a lost time accident.

630
00:42:09,701 --> 00:42:15,700
I think we only had three lost time accidents during the entire project.

631
00:42:15,701 --> 00:42:17,540
entire project.

632
00:42:17,541 --> 00:42:22,800
The arch may fill its millions of visitors with wonder, but it also fills its thousands

633
00:42:22,801 --> 00:42:28,800
of construction workers, engineers and designers with a special pride.

634
00:42:28,801 --> 00:42:33,680
Everybody that I've talked to that worked on the arch talks about it being one of the

635
00:42:33,681 --> 00:42:37,160
most phenomenal projects they ever worked on.

636
00:42:37,161 --> 00:42:41,080
And they say, you just can't imagine the reaction you get from people when you say you actually

637
00:42:41,081 --> 00:42:42,080
worked on the arch.

638
00:42:42,081 --> 00:42:44,600
is something that you can only do one time.

639
00:42:45,120 --> 00:42:46,120
It's a part of history.

640
00:42:47,760 --> 00:42:52,600
I would say that it is one of the most extraordinary images

641
00:42:52,601 --> 00:42:55,240
in the 20th century construction,

642
00:42:56,060 --> 00:43:00,280
the most appropriate technical expression of our time.

643
00:43:02,480 --> 00:43:05,540
Creative in design and aesthetic in appearance,

644
00:43:06,400 --> 00:43:10,400
the St. Louis Arch is a monument to those with a vision.

